@meta
id: 09-10
title: Stop just short with dt
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-motion
par: 16
minutes: 4
vscode: select from the plus to just before the semicolon, Delete
open: totals.ts

@brief
The surcharge has been folded into shipping upstream, so adding it here
charges the customer twice.

    const total = order.subtotal + order.shipping + order.surcharge;

Drop the surcharge from the sum. The semicolon stays, the rest of the
line stays, and there should be no stray space left before the
semicolon.

@keys
| `t;` | forward to just before the next semicolon |
| `dt;` | delete from the cursor up to, not including, the semicolon |
| `2f+` | forward to the second plus |

@hints
`d$` would take the semicolon with it. You want to stop one character
short of something, which is exactly what `t` is for.
--
`dt;` deletes up to but not including the semicolon. Start it from the
space before the second `+` and the spacing comes out right.
--
`j`, `2f+` to land on the second plus, `h` to step back onto the space,
then `dt;`.

@solution
    j 2f+ h dt; :wq<Enter>

`dt;` is the single most useful operator-motion pair in day-to-day code,
because a statement almost always ends in a semicolon or a brace and
almost never ends exactly where you want to stop deleting. `d$` takes the
terminator with it and you have to type it back. `dt;` leaves it.

The same shape covers most of a working day:

    dt)     kill the rest of an argument list, keep the bracket
    dt,     kill one argument, keep the separator
    ct)     retype the rest of the call
    dt'     clear a string, keep its quote

`f` and `t` differ only in whether the target comes along. Deleting: `t`
nearly always. Jumping: `f` nearly always, because `t` leaves you one
short of where you were looking.

@start totals.ts
export function orderTotal(order: Order) {
  const total = order.subtotal + order.shipping + order.surcharge;

  return Math.round(total * 100) / 100;
}

@expected totals.ts
export function orderTotal(order: Order) {
  const total = order.subtotal + order.shipping;

  return Math.round(total * 100) / 100;
}
